  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1211 AM EDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis and Forecast:\n\nSfc high pressure begins to nose in from the west today and tonight \ndue to upstream ridging aloft. Thus, dry conditions will be more \ncommon than not, although with the lingering low level moisture \nwould not be entirely surprised to see some patchy drizzle \nearly, mainly near Lake Michigan. That being said, probably not \ngoing to inject any officially in the forecast due to the spotty\nnature, if it happens at all. Northwest winds continue through \nthe day today as well, as we remain socked in with clouds. Does \nnot look like a huge signal for fog tonight either with high \npressure drifting off to the northeast across the Canadian \nShield and the potential for non-zero sfc winds, but we'll see \nabout that going forward. Although relatively uneventful today \nand tonight, we'll be able to sniff the next weather system(s) \nupstream.\n\nBy Thursday, two distinct features will take aim at northern \nMI. The first feature is an upper low emerging out of the \nSouthwest and Central Plains, that slowly moves northeast with \ntime on Thursday while weakening. Another more robust feature \nwill be diving out of Sask Canada down into the Upper Midwest \nand eventually Great Lakes region. Southern stream piece of \nenergy gets absorbed into the more robust feature coming out of \nCanada, which will inject some decent moisture into northern \nMichigan (~1.0-1.4\" PWATs, anomalous for this time of year). \nWith the combined lift from the stout upper trough to the \nnorthwest and the sfc reflection from the upper low to the \nsouthwest coupled with this moisture, a steady and healthy rain \nis upon northern Michigan (beneficial for areas within D1/D2 \ndrought like the E UP). Current deterministic guidance shows \naround 0.25-0.75\" of rain generally, with ENS guidance\nsuggesting a medium potential (~40-70%) for 0.50\" of rain or \nmore across a chunk of our area.\n\nAs the two pieces of upper level energy merge/phase and the jet \nstream aloft intensifies, the sfc reflection will follow suit off to \nour east. Thus, pressure gradient will increase through the day on \nFriday with ~35 knot H850 winds overspreading the area late. \nLittle less impressive signal compared to yesterday, due to the \nway the energies phase (main energy also noticeably farther to \nthe south) and intensity/timing of the low level jet, but windy \nconditions are still expected on Friday, 25 to 40 mph gusts certainly\npossible, potential for higher near the Lake Michigan \nshoreline. Nevertheless, we'll see how this trends in the coming\ndays.\n\nPrecipitation chances largely relinquish through the weekend and \ninto early next week as heights fill back in across northern \nMichigan and temperatures turn milder (60s again). Brief piece \nof energy well to the north across Canada beats the ridging down\nto some degree late weekend into early next week. The only \nconsequence for northern Michigan will be an increased pressure \ngradient due to a wound up low pressure system near Hudson Bay.\nThus, an increase in winds can be expected once more, looks \nlike mainly Sunday at this time.",
